---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Demo from "../../../components/Demo.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { backLink, pagination, header, toc, articleLayout } from "../../../examples/navigation";
import { url } from "../../../lib/urls";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Navigation"
  description="Make the current location and the next step clear. Use real destinations, native links, and one understandable navigation structure."
  section="Components"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/components/navigation.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="back-links">A predictable way back</h2>
    <p>
      <code>lf-back-link</code> is a small navigational link, not a browser-history command. Point it
      to a meaningful parent page so it works even when someone arrives directly from an external link.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Demo id="navigation-back" title="A real parent destination" code={backLink} />

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="pagination">Pagination is still navigation</h2>
      <p>
        Use <code>lf-pagination</code> on a named <code>nav</code>. Mark the active link with <code
          >aria-current="page"</code
        >. Render an unavailable next or previous direction as noninteractive text rather than a
        dead link.
      </p>
      <p>
        These demo links navigate between component guides. The current page is the final item in
        this small example; no pretend dataset is loaded.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo
      id="navigation-pagination"
      title="Native links and a current-page state"
      code={pagination}
    />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        If your application loads results dynamically, update the URL, page state, focus, and result
        announcements appropriately. The CSS component does not implement a router or pagination
        controller.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="headers">A shared site header</h2>
      <p>
        <code>lf-header</code>, <code>lf-header__inner</code>,
        <code>lf-brand</code>, and <code>lf-nav</code> form the existing header vocabulary. Use a short
        primary navigation with meaningful link names. The header on this documentation is a working example
        with website-specific spacing and responsive choices.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock id="navigation-header" code={header} label="Site header" />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        The header classes do not implement a mobile menu. A short link list may wrap; a longer one
        needs a considered disclosure pattern. This site uses native <code>details</code> for its mobile
        documentation menu, so it also works without JavaScript.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>lf-footer</code>, <code>lf-footer__inner</code>, and
        <code>lf-footer__links</code> provide the matching footer structure. Name navigation landmarks
        distinctly when a page has more than one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="article-toc">Optional article navigation</h2>
      <p>
        The TOC controller finds <code>h2</code> and <code>h3</code> headings inside
        <code>data-lineframe-prose</code>, creates links, and tracks the current section. At least
        two headings are required. Supply stable IDs when you want durable, shareable links; missing
        IDs are generated.
      </p>
      <p>
        This compact preview keeps the TOC above the example text. It does not introduce another
        documentation rail. On narrow screens, use the toggle to open the section links.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo
      id="navigation-toc"
      class="docs-toc-demo"
      title="A live, generated table of contents"
      code={toc}
    />
    <noscript>
      <p class="docs-inline-note">
        JavaScript is off. The example article remains readable without a TOC.
      </p>
    </noscript>
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h3 id="article-layout">A standalone article layout</h3>
      <p>
        For a full article page, use the existing article layout below. It puts navigation on the
        left on wide screens and above the prose on smaller ones. The initially hidden TOC stays out
        of the way without JavaScript.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock id="navigation-article" code={articleLayout} label="Standalone article" />
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        Keep <code>data-lineframe-article</code>,
        <code>data-lineframe-prose</code>, <code>data-lineframe-toc</code>, and
        <code>data-lineframe-toc-list</code> in the same article structure. The generated links use <code
          >aria-current="location"</code
        >, which is different from a site link’s current-page state.
      </p>
      <p>
        The CDN script initializes the TOC automatically. Module consumers call
        <code>initToc()</code> after rendering the article and clean it up on teardown. See <a
          href={url("docs/javascript/#toc-api")}>the lifecycle API</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
